In my opinion, polypiling as it stands right now is too powerful.
With minimal effort, a wand of polymorph can be used to generate reams
of useful items from junk.  This can be taken advantage of quite
viciously (with a little knowledge about item types).

I want to highlight this by doing a quick comparison: wand of
polymorph vs. magic marker.  There are a number of assumptions made in
this example, but the intent is to show that in this specific case,
polypiling is significantly overpowered.


The Scenario
-You want to create scrolls of enchant armor.

Assumptions
1) You have access to one wand of polymorph and one magic marker.
2) Both items are unused at the start of the scenario.
3) You have access to a sufficient number of blessed blank/junk
scrolls.
4) You are playing a Tourist and have the Platinum Yendorian Express
Card.
5) When polypiling, you are zapping 20 scrolls at a time, grouped in
piles of four.


Quick facts on the items in question:

Magic Marker
-.12% of all randomly generated items
-average number of charges: 79.5
-number of recharges allowed: 1 (set to 1:50)

Wand of Polymorph
-.18% of all randomly generated items
-average number of charges: 4.5
-average number of successful recharges before explosion: 5.556 (cube
root of (7^3 / 2))
-average number of zaps per wand: 4.5 * 6.556 = 29.502

Scroll of Enchant Armor
-6.3% of all randomly generated scrolls
-average number of marker charges required to write: 11.5


How many EA scrolls can we get from the average marker?
1) Write an average of 6.913 EA scrolls with the initial 79.5 charges.
2) Recharge the marker to (1:50) with the PYEC.
3) Write 4.348 more scrolls of EA.
Average # of total scrolls produced: 11.261

How many EA scrolls can we get from the average wand of polymorph?
1) Zap (20 scrolls * .063 chance of each scroll becoming enchant
armor) = 1.26 EA scrolls per zap
2) Recharge wand as necessary with the PYEC until explosion
Average # of total scrolls produced: 1.26 * 29.502 = 37.173


A random wand of polymorph will produce an average of 3.301 times as
many enchant armor scrolls (alternatively, an average of 8.937 zaps of
a wand of polymorph will produce as many EA scrolls as the average
magic marker).

(Credit goes to several spolers for the hard numbers on item
generation, numbers of charges, and chance of wands exploding.)


Some Generalization and Other Considerations
-***polypiling 20 items at a time causes some items to shudder and
disappear (I couldn't find these numbers in the spoilers, if a source
diver would be willing to plug them into my math here I'd really
appreciate it).***
-magic markers guarantee that you're getting what you want.
-magic markers make if significantly easier to produce cursed or
uncursed scrolls, should you want them (genocide comes to mind).
-wands of polymorph are 50% more common than magic markers.
-(the big one) polypiling scrolls/spellbooks/tools may create many
other useful types of scrolls/spellbooks/tools in addition to your
target type.

I think magic markers are quite well balanced items in terms of
rarity, number of charges, and the number of scrolls/spellbooks you
can typically produce with them.  Now, consider that you'll find 3
wands of polymorph for every 2 magic markers, and that you can use
your typical wand of polymorph to generate 3-4 times (or more) as many
useful items.  Sounds like a broken item to me.


Potential Ways to Balance Wands of Polymorph
-have polymorphing scrolls of blank paper/blank spellbooks produce
blank scrolls/spellbooks
-increased likelihood of piles shuddering
-increased likelihood of items resisting polymorph
-flat rate percentage chance of an item being destroyed each time it's
polymorphed (maybe a 50%--15% slider based on luck)


Please point out any errors in my calculations; I want this to be
right.  Interested to hear others' thoughts on the subject.


